{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":299.9800109863281,"text":"in these desks. Yeah, and one thing, just briefly on that as well, is just like the reproducibility of things that have been found. Like how, you know, if... So I guess if I think more of the SAS model, it's like, well, this thing has been found, it's very clear, like here's the steps to recreate it, for example. I get the impression that the AI found vulnerabilities that sort of can tell you what was found and what the impact might be, but actually then for someone to reproduce that, I think, isn't that a little bit more challenging? Yeah, it is. Just I was on a call before we started recording the podcast. That was one of the issues that we discussed, that part of building a good AI system today, like every AI system, is how do you create your evals, your evaluations and benchmarks and to understand what you're doing well. And sometimes what happens when you make a change to your system, whether you can actually reproduce the same processes that you saw before. First, I think that we have to remember that LLMs, there is, of course, some random factor there since they are based on probabilistic processes. But our goal is to try and make them as deterministic as possible. And I think the honest answer is that we're not there yet. And we see that what we're trying to build, when I talked about harnesses before, is really to make sure that regardless of the probabilistic nature of the LLM, we want to make sure that it actually reproduces the... can run it deterministically. Of course, when you are a vendor that creates products, it's hard to sell something that might give different answers. Honestly, I see more tolerance, I guess, to this randomality that is out there. I think that if you look back four years, it was something that no one accepted. And I think now people are getting used to AI systems that give you different answers. So there is maybe slightly more tolerance than what we used to see before in cybersecurity. But in the end, in cybersecurity, yeah, you can have these different results for different runs. And part of what we're doing with our engines is trying to reproduce the issue multiple times. We have to remember that some vulnerabilities also have this probabilistic nature, memory vulnerabilities, vulnerabilities that just... Yeah, I mean, vulnerability can... You can run the exploit, or just one out of ten, on average, just one out of ten runs will succeed. So you have two different mechanisms here that are probabilistic, and that makes it even harder to run. But yeah, it's part of the challenges that we have today. It's part of the work. I think also like the Frontier Labs, they invest great efforts in making the models more deterministic. Yeah, and I think this is it. I mean, it goes outside security, but just keeping it within security, I think the unlock here is people are still... They're more willing to accept the less deterministic aspect on the basis that they'll still get quite a lot of things that would just never have been found if this had to be a completely deterministic system. So that's what you get. You get the... Yes, it's a larger pool of things to kind of sift through, but equally, some of those literally have been found to be some like crazily problematic things that SAS haven't picked up for many years, for example. So that makes sense why sort of tolerance is a little bit higher, especially at the moment, which is super interesting. As we sort of like cruise to the, I guess sort of to the end of the episode, I mean, I really like the framing again from your article, which is like, it's a very positive framing, which is that AI is available to attackers as much as it is on the defense side. But the defense side is looking better, basically. And yeah, I mean, again, if I look at the zero-day clock, I really encourage anyone listening to go check it out. First of all, just to go back on what I said around, we are in 2026, apparently we are actually down to two hours on the time to exploit. So that's just the fact we're not at one yet. So that's why it hadn't been ticked off. But I look at the chart again, it's like, oh, wow, we're down to two hours. So it's not only a day, but it's down to two hours. But the other thing which is interesting is that the exploit rate, so this is like percentage of all published CVEs that are confirmed exploited in the wild versus CVE volume has actually dropped, which kind of backs up, I think, your sort of positive thesis there, right? Where like actually now in 2026, it's down to like, the exploit rate is down to 0.25%. That's like, just to give some relative terms, that's down from 2.2% in 2021. So yeah, that's really interesting. That does kind of suggest that AI being available is actually improving things. Yeah, maybe by the time the episode is over, or, you know, it will be, the exploit time will be shorter. But I think that's it.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":1.7599999904632568,"text":" in these desks.","tokens":[50364,294,613,730,1694,13,50452],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":1.7999999523162842,"end":4.960000038146973,"text":" Yeah, and one thing, just briefly on that as well,","tokens":[50454,865,11,293,472,551,11,445,10515,322,300,382,731,11,50612],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":5.0,"end":10.0600004196167,"text":" is just like the reproducibility of things that have been found.","tokens":[50614,307,445,411,264,11408,537,39802,295,721,300,362,668,1352,13,50867],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":3,"seek":0,"start":10.100000381469727,"end":12.760000228881836,"text":" Like how, you know, if...","tokens":[50869,1743,577,11,291,458,11,498,485,51002],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":4,"seek":0,"start":12.800000190734863,"end":15.460000038146973,"text":" So I guess if I think more of the SAS model,","tokens":[51004,407,286,2041,498,286,519,544,295,264,33441,2316,11,51137],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":5,"seek":0,"start":15.5,"end":18.059999465942383,"text":" it's like, well, this thing has been found, it's very clear,","tokens":[51139,309,311,411,11,731,11,341,551,575,668,1352,11,309,311,588,1850,11,51267],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":6,"seek":0,"start":18.100000381469727,"end":20.600000381469727,"text":" like here's the steps to recreate it, for example.","tokens":[51269,411,510,311,264,4439,281,25833,309,11,337,1365,13,51394],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":7,"seek":0,"start":20.639999389648438,"end":24.139999389648438,"text":" I get the impression that the AI found vulnerabilities","tokens":[51396,286,483,264,9995,300,264,7318,1352,37633,51571],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":8,"seek":0,"start":24.15999984741211,"end":26.860000610351562,"text":" that sort of can tell you what was found","tokens":[51572,300,1333,295,393,980,291,437,390,1352,51707],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.369384765625,"compression_ratio":1.6988416910171509,"no_speech_prob":0.054913002997636795},{"id":9,"seek":0,"start":26.899999618530273,"end":28.899999618530273,"text":" and what the impact might be,","tokens":[51709,293,437,264,2712,1062,312,11,51809],"temperature":0.0,"avg_log